","related_refs":["673a9872b715a5f0f9080ecde6ef17f13ee6b5fe5e1f04f615e9e14a9a073346","268bf8aaf33529f3939ef2f9b145d03a719924228e01b5939fb0a1cd890ff698","58b49350d38c98fbc34417a51cc47de21d6465cbbbc90bc9fc0c5c3e7a3ad2dc","07071810050f0cc00b099ec62f6286ef3800b5314b5228db62804299b9cfda64","7d1ee64a4b2079606baa215f6747e596d55615a3b938502fad115543796a6888","48afe33acd70f9a567cf4ae020e7e3be5f6b43c1ec98104f5eaca38e655d77b9","6a95e6c7a4e9c26e46092d64063756fd1884dc67e54b6d9bbfe6b06367f186b0","6f1a7c780e46680f0f6c157cc674d5647be1ec1b5c5e4cf33f13a4ba7c081f2c","d0c1cb17e452843e6791556aadc0e9397e48b30084ac57dff386ef2d4e4a3e77","090a8fee07ca4d3156adba977154197fc20207f3d956711a724d6094f7d604f4","69e5961fb15f3aa92a3ed26ae043cc46bac97208fcc6a846dc69cb9219a0a6c3","dc538880f41eb17f00721078c588cbc2b137da44d1c268b2b34e492f1330f7b3","491f1422ce2e249132473b9fac078025a2a5d95c6e79d6d15e0b4526ab367af0","c5976d829ec8a532fb1e679fa35b147709cdffc865f8e08008831f5f31a5c4b1","e51f3c9555120a6ced5092ff3539d4d80b80eeb61fe56ccb74b6be52df719791","213f658f3785db05d3b2fe53de668eb7ab50011449a96ccd9ce2f7be951148b9"],"related_total":18,"schema":"bridge.process-observation.v1","statement":"At formal sequence 471, problem question-209e3154673cd752fd6dc26d2ddbbf90 retained an unresolved rivalry among 18 positions.","subject_ref":"question-209e3154673cd752fd6dc26d2ddbbf90"}],"retrieval_receipt_ref":"sha256:b76abfa60b0ff08256e2bd92997c74972f4422143f0b6b46fec459cfcc05d0ac","schema":"bridge.catalog.v3"},"composition_request":{"desired_length_chars":16384,"formatting_profile":"plain","maximum_sections":4,"output_target":"answer"},"evidence_budget_chars":24000,"evidence_pack":{"argued_refutations":[],"catalog_contract":"bridge.catalog.v3","catalog_items":[{"excerpt":"[673a9872b715] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The safest protocol is an append-only intent-and-seal log with epoch fencing; it dominates lease-based alternatives because it records causality directly rather than inferring it from timeouts, and the exact durable records are the intent entry and the seal entry.\nMECHANISM: Before acquiring the workspace, the process appends an intent record (problem_id, epoch, workspace_path, process_id) to a durable append-only log. Upon completion, it appends a seal record containing the output evidence hash. Reclamation performs a log-scan: any workspace with an intent but no matching seal, whose holding process has been confirmed dead by a liveness oracle, is reclaimed. The causal mechanism is that the log provides a recoverable total order of intent and seal events, so displacement is always preceded by a visible gap (intent without seal), never silent. Adj","handle":"A1","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"673a9872b715a5f0f9080ecde6ef17f13ee6b5fe5e1f04f615e9e14a9a073346"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 673a9872b715a5f0f9080ecde6ef17f13ee6b5fe5e1f04f615e9e14a9a073346 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P1","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:bfba1336a665ee4de43107d471a484c525a7fcd7d6d2a7f6c39ce3cee05ac2c2"},{"excerpt":"[268bf8aaf335] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: A content-addressed derivation cache with durable provenance anchors achieves equivalent safety without requiring a global log, because evidence identity is decoupled from workspace location; the exact durable record is the (problem_id, input_hash, output_hash) provenance anchor.\nMECHANISM: The deterministic workspace implies reproducibility. The protocol durably records a provenance anchor mapping problem_id to (input_hash, output_hash) before declaring evidence complete. The actual workspace directory is treated as ephemeral scratch. Reclamation is always safe because there is no completed evidence in the workspace—only a cache that can be rebuilt. Displacement of the workspace is therefore never displacement of evidence. The causal mechanism is the cryptographic commitment in the provenance anchor, which binds the evidence to its causal history","handle":"A2","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"268bf8aaf33529f3939ef2f9b145d03a719924228e01b5939fb0a1cd890ff698"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 268bf8aaf33529f3939ef2f9b145d03a719924228e01b5939fb0a1cd890ff698 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P2","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:10be7aa645a5f2871bdcf7317ae9ad78f09604541474f923af7db92d2b3cf317"},{"excerpt":"[58b49350d38c] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Skeptical best-effort: absolute 'never' is impossible without synchronous consensus; the safest practical protocol is a durable lease table with explicit revocation tokens, adjudicated against log-based protocols by its superior availability under partition.\nMECHANISM: A lease manager durably records (workspace_id, holder_pid, issued_timestamp, ttl, revocation_flag). The process must heartbeat to renew. Reclamation queries the lease table and a process liveness oracle; if both confirm expiry or death, it issues a revocation token and reclaims. The causal mechanism is timeout-driven state transition. It adjudicates against the intent-seal log: while the log protocol is safer in consistent histories, it fails closed under partition (stalls reclamation), whereas the lease protocol can make progress with bounded uncertainty. Therefore, where evidence ","handle":"A3","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"58b49350d38c98fbc34417a51cc47de21d6465cbbbc90bc9fc0c5c3e7a3ad2dc"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 58b49350d38c98fbc34417a51cc47de21d6465cbbbc90bc9fc0c5c3e7a3ad2dc had formal status accepted.","handle":"P3","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:1a8cecfdd9fc274fed3e0237d43a9439b51192178bf82ec355645134a94fe72d"},{"excerpt":"[07071810050f] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: A unifying generational tri-state manifest (active/staging/sealed) with downstream dependency pinning subsumes both log and lease protocols by making causal dependencies explicit in the workspace metadata itself; the exact durable record is the manifest containing generation, state, and downstream references.\nMECHANISM: The durable record is a manifest per problem containing generation number, state, and a list of downstream evidence refs that consume this workspace. State transitions: active → staging (completion in progress) → sealed (completed). Reclamation first marks staging workspaces as 'zombie', waits for downstream dependency resolution, then reclaims only if no downstream ref exists and the holding process is dead. The causal mechanism is reference counting over the derivation graph. Adjudication: this protocol is safer than pure lease o","handle":"A4","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"07071810050f0cc00b099ec62f6286ef3800b5314b5228db62804299b9cfda64"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 07071810050f0cc00b099ec62f6286ef3800b5314b5228db62804299b9cfda64 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P4","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:2f7b6291c39f0d1006f7280941d27f8561b8c97806513b787d3c6785d3a583ab"},{"excerpt":"[7d1ee64a4b20] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Crash-only software with resurrection logs: safety is achieved by never distinguishing between crash and restart; the durable record is a birth certificate that enables idempotent workspace reconstruction rather than hazardous reclamation.\nMECHANISM: On every startup, the process writes a birth certificate (problem_id, deterministic_seed, workspace_path, incarnation_number) to durable storage. All writes to the workspace are idempotent and logged in a redo log. 'Reclamation' is simply a new process incarnation reading the birth certificate and replaying the log to determine if evidence was already completed. If completed, the workspace is re-adopted; if not, it is reconstructed. The causal mechanism is deterministic replay: the workspace is not a resource to reclaim but a function of the durable birth certificate and log. This protocol never displ","handle":"A5","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"7d1ee64a4b2079606baa215f6747e596d55615a3b938502fad115543796a6888"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 7d1ee64a4b2079606baa215f6747e596d55615a3b938502fad115543796a6888 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P5","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:d83b6174cdc0b1231d9c70bcd31867d53a73eab531c3416d9df61cc8769ed3e8"},{"excerpt":"[48afe33acd70] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: An epistemic, data-centric protocol using a distributed Merkle witness tree shifts safety from process liveness to content acknowledgment; the exact durable records are Merkle roots and inclusion proofs.\nMECHANISM: Rather than tracking process liveness, the protocol requires that any completed evidence be hashed and that hash inserted into a Merkle tree witnessed by multiple observers. Reclamation performs a 'corpse examination': it hashes the orphaned workspace and checks inclusion in the Merkle tree. If present, evidence is completed and must not be displaced. If absent, reclamation proceeds. The causal mechanism shifts from process-centric to data-centric: a workspace is safe not because its creator is alive but because its content is globally acknowledged. Adjudication: where evidence shows observer crash rates are lower than process crash rat","handle":"A6","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"48afe33acd70f9a567cf4ae020e7e3be5f6b43c1ec98104f5eaca38e655d77b9"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 48afe33acd70f9a567cf4ae020e7e3be5f6b43c1ec98104f5eaca38e655d77b9 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P6","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:23775967e310c83b4c1153e818652f1c6cf8891cec90638817cdf1b2a0963c7c"},{"excerpt":"[6a95e6c7a4e9] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The safest deterministic reclamation protocol is an append-only intent-and-seal log with epoch fencing and monotonic-read witnesses; it dominates lease-based rivals because it records completion before reclamation can observe death, and the exact durable record must contain the ordered tuple (problem-id, content-addressed evidence-hash, seal-epoch, upstream-dependency-hashes, holder-intent-bit).\nMECHANISM: Before releasing the workspace, the holder appends a seal entry to a causally-ordered log. The reclaimer scans backward from the log tail for a seal entry matching the workspace problem-id; if none is visible in the reclaimer's causal cut, reclamation is forbidden. Epoch fencing prevents a zombie holder from sealing after the workspace has been reclaimed and reissued. The required durable record is therefore the seal entry itself plus the log's ","handle":"A7","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"6a95e6c7a4e9c26e46092d64063756fd1884dc67e54b6d9bbfe6b06367f186b0"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 6a95e6c7a4e9c26e46092d64063756fd1884dc67e54b6d9bbfe6b06367f186b0 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P7","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:16b9d8c52701a7b5955db6e724b1b9810ce9589d09ce8ebc85616678719d841b"},{"excerpt":"[6f1a7c780e46] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: A content-addressed derivation cache with durable provenance anchors achieves equivalent safety without a global log, provided the anchor record includes the full dependency closure, a tombstone-resistant witness attestation, and a seal-of-derivation signed by the workspace holder.\nMECHANISM: Completed evidence is externalized as a content-addressed object. The holder writes a provenance anchor to a durable, replicated key-value store keyed by problem-id, containing (content-hash, input-hashes, timestamp-witness, holder-epoch). Reclamation only proceeds if a causal cut shows no anchor for the problem-id and the holder's heartbeat lease has expired. Because the evidence is content-addressed, displacement is detectable: recompute yields the same hash, revealing a silent displacement after the fact.","handle":"A8","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"6f1a7c780e46680f0f6c157cc674d5647be1ec1b5c5e4cf33f13a4ba7c081f2c"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 6f1a7c780e46680f0f6c157cc674d5647be1ec1b5c5e4cf33f13a4ba7c081f2c had formal status accepted.","handle":"P8","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:3377faacc2abf48099705098433ed400a95d029bdab972a9ea43fa189781df29"},{"excerpt":"[d0c1cb17e452] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Treating the workspace as a causal broadcast medium where every write emits a content-hash witness into a Merkle DAG shifts reclamation from a liveness test to a data-availability query; safety is preserved if and only if the protocol durably records the root hash of the problem-specific witness subtree and a quorum certificate before any reclamation decision.\nMECHANISM: The holder asynchronously publishes workspace state transitions as Merkle leaves; completion is marked by sealing the subtree root into a globally visible DAG node. The reclaimer queries DAG reachability rather than process status. The required durable record is the set of DAG root hashes witnessed by a quorum of reclaimer-adjacent nodes, ensuring that even if the original holder crashes without a terminal local record, the evidence persists in the DAG fabric.","handle":"A9","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"d0c1cb17e452843e6791556aadc0e9397e48b30084ac57dff386ef2d4e4a3e77"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact d0c1cb17e452843e6791556aadc0e9397e48b30084ac57dff386ef2d4e4a3e77 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P9","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:6c965f3162e9efe0de2d7743b240b8ee9771a3b5a6fcad7d4fe1150e2fba66fb"},{"excerpt":"[090a8fee07ca] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: No single protocol is intrinsically safest; rather, safety is the comparative latency invariant that seal-visibility latency must be bounded above by failure-detection latency, and the exact durable record must be the pair of causal timestamps (seal-emission, death-detection) together with their happens-before proof.\nMECHANISM: Adjudication between log-based and lease-based rivals proceeds by measuring worst-case seal-visibility latency under asynchrony. The protocol whose seal can be observed by the reclaimer strictly before the reclaimer concludes death is safe. The durable record must therefore include a vector clock or hybrid logical clock establishing that the seal event precedes the reclamation event in the happens-before relation, making the safety property verifiable from the record alone.","handle":"A10","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"090a8fee07ca4d3156adba977154197fc20207f3d956711a724d6094f7d604f4"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 090a8fee07ca4d3156adba977154197fc20207f3d956711a724d6094f7d604f4 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P10","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:c2d4cbfc99c005d08686c6cd1d471c8d7ae5f36d9fcaed3f3d398ee004060d46"},{"excerpt":"[69e5961fb15f] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: A hybrid protocol combining epoch-locked workspaces with a two-phase seal witness—comprising a durable intent-to-seal record followed by a finalization record—prevents the spurious displacement observed in lease protocols by ensuring the reclaimer must observe both the absence of intent and the confirmation of death before reclamation.\nMECHANISM: When approaching completion, the holder writes a durable intent record containing (problem-id, proposed-content-hash, holder-epoch) to a replicated intent store. After terminal flush of evidence to durable storage, it writes a finalize record. The reclaimer must verify: (a) no un-finalized intent record exists for the problem-id, and (b) the holder's epoch lease is expired and not renewed. The required durable record is therefore the ordered pair (intent, finalize) in a holder-local but replicated store, ","handle":"A11","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"69e5961fb15f3aa92a3ed26ae043cc46bac97208fcc6a846dc69cb9219a0a6c3"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 69e5961fb15f3aa92a3ed26ae043cc46bac97208fcc6a846dc69cb9219a0a6c3 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P11","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:22dbdad8a869f94eab470eed4584aa2f47b0fbc8e4353299ee118795d6f2bf92"},{"excerpt":"[dc538880f41e] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Because the workspace is deterministic per-problem, the safest reclamation protocol is to treat reclamation as a recompute-then-compare operation rather than a metadata check; the exact durable record required is simply the problem identifier and the content-addressed expectation of the output, and adjudication must favor protocols that validate evidence by reproduction over protocols that infer state from holder liveness.\nMECHANISM: The reclaimer recomputes the problem in a fresh workspace and compares the result to any cached evidence; if they match, the original workspace is dispensable and any displacement is harmless because the evidence is recoverable. If they do not match, the workspace is retained. This shifts the safety burden from crash detection to determinism verification. The required durable record is (problem-id, deterministic-funct","handle":"A12","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"dc538880f41eb17f00721078c588cbc2b137da44d1c268b2b34e492f1330f7b3"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact dc538880f41eb17f00721078c588cbc2b137da44d1c268b2b34e492f1330f7b3 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P12","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:ddbd48e3fb91beda95542afa2d10ce16f2f5057d1935c318de43e4672d79a6a7"},{"excerpt":"[491f1422ce2e] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The safest reclamation protocol is a content-addressed seal-before-grant dependency: workspace reclamation for a deterministic problem ID must be gated on the visibility of a durable, content-addressed seal of all completed evidence in a logically separate, higher-durability ledger, and the workspace itself must carry no authority to overwrite evidence without this external seal proof.\nMECHANISM: Before any workspace is reclaimed, the protocol queries a durable seal ledger by problem ID. If a content-addressed evidence hash exists under that problem ID, reclamation proceeds only after cryptographic verification of the hash. The process emits heartbeats, but these merely manage liveness, not displacement authority. The durable record must contain: (1) the problem-workspace binding, (2) the content-addressed evidence hash in the seal ledger, and (3)","handle":"A13","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"491f1422ce2e249132473b9fac078025a2a5d95c6e79d6d15e0b4526ab367af0"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 491f1422ce2e249132473b9fac078025a2a5d95c6e79d6d15e0b4526ab367af0 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P13","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:db7d60b4b74dc004f5b31600fa49c3d18a5c465d345d4445e2a39f66e68b7f79"},{"excerpt":"[c5976d829ec8] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: Log-based protocols are provably safer than lease-based rivals because they enforce the causal latency invariant that seal-visibility latency is strictly bounded above by failure-detection latency, whereas lease-based protocols conflate death detection with displacement permission and therefore violate this invariant under partial synchrony.\nMECHANISM: Completed evidence is sealed into an append-only shared log before workspace release. Reclamation reads the log tail for the problem ID; if no seal is visible, reclamation must stall indefinitely. The durable record must contain: (1) the log sequence number, (2) the content-addressed evidence hash at that sequence, and (3) a signed checkpoint of the log prefix. Adjudication favors the log because it externalizes the happens-before relation: the log records completion before reclamation can observe d","handle":"A14","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"c5976d829ec8a532fb1e679fa35b147709cdffc865f8e08008831f5f31a5c4b1"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact c5976d829ec8a532fb1e679fa35b147709cdffc865f8e08008831f5f31a5c4b1 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P14","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:e5f33d577a294795163190969ffbccb8805419fb7e904380b751649ac45a4f24"},{"excerpt":"[e51f3c955512] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The safest protocol collapses the workspace into the transaction log by making evidence completion and durability recording a single atomic operation on a shared substrate, rendering reclamation a cache eviction that cannot silently displace evidence because evidence never resided exclusively in the workspace.\nMECHANISM: The deterministic per-problem workspace is treated as a pure cache; the source of truth is a transactional seal store. A process 'completes' evidence by committing a transaction containing (problem_id, content_hash, workspace_nonce). Reclamation is simply 'reclaim the cache line' and requires no additional local durable record because displacement is impossible—the workspace holds only ephemeral state. What must be durably recorded is: the problem-workspace mapping, the sealed evidence hash, and the transaction commit timestamp. T","handle":"A15","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"e51f3c9555120a6ced5092ff3539d4d80b80eeb61fe56ccb74b6be52df719791"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact e51f3c9555120a6ced5092ff3539d4d80b80eeb61fe56ccb74b6be52df719791 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P15","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:dbe0d7b2fc35b17b13f0bed740959a5d5d6188f9617f5ff253183a594dbb8416"},{"excerpt":"[213f658f3785] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: A lease-based protocol augmented with monotonic deterministic epochs and non-replayable tombstones can achieve equivalent safety to log-based protocols while providing superior reclamation latency, adjudicating the rivalry by showing that leases are unsafe only when they lack lineage awareness.\nMECHANISM: The workspace is tagged with a monotonic epoch nonce derived from a durable epoch ledger. Completed evidence is recorded as (problem_id, epoch, content_hash, tombstone_flag). Reclamation increments the epoch and writes a tombstone; any restarted process detects an epoch mismatch and must reconcile before overwriting. The durable record must contain: (1) the current epoch for the problem ID, (2) the content-addressed evidence hash bound to that epoch, and (3) the tombstone record proving reclamation occurred. This adjudicates lease vs log by demon","handle":"A16","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"213f658f3785db05d3b2fe53de668eb7ab50011449a96ccd9ce2f7be951148b9"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 213f658f3785db05d3b2fe53de668eb7ab50011449a96ccd9ce2f7be951148b9 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P16","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:d760fc00b0b2ae8c4c82387d53151562a3a5dfa5d6734e8fb8096eb1980decf6"},{"excerpt":"[51d9cb994e84] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: The workspace must be treated as a causal broadcast medium emitting content-hash pings into an external Merkle DAG, making reclamation a data-availability query rather than a liveness check, since a crashed process cannot self-witness its own death.\nMECHANISM: Drawing from the broadcast-medium inversion: every write to the workspace emits a content-hash 'ping' into a durable Merkle DAG before the write is acknowledged. Reclamation asks not 'is the process alive?' but 'has any node ever witnessed the final evidence state for this problem ID?' The durable record must contain: (1) the Merkle root of witnessed hashes, (2) the problem-workspace binding as a DAG node, and (3) a witness quorum attestation. This directly addresses the crash-without-terminal-record by removing the process from the critical path of safety; the process merely proposes eviden","handle":"A17","kind":"formal_artifact","ref":"51d9cb994e84827ac63665ea3361fbf824c28ddc447f11e9e744760f45b63850"},{"excerpt":"At formal sequence 471, artifact 51d9cb994e84827ac63665ea3361fbf824c28ddc447f11e9e744760f45b63850 had formal status accepted.","handle":"P17","kind":"process_observation","ref":"sha256:9e6e1aa64e37ea5810a9cbdcee20b8dca1721ad30c266ce59c4a2b5b5425185c"},{"excerpt":"[d821832dd46f] (school: school-2)\nCLAIM: No purely local reclamation protocol can satisfy the requirement to never silently displace completed evidence when a crash leaves no terminal record; therefore the only safe protocol is not a local lease/log rivalry but a distributed consensus over external witnesses, and the question of adjudicating single-node protocols is founded on a false premise.\n