OSITIONS (accepted after criticism) ==\n[673a9872b715] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The safest protocol is an append-only intent-and-seal log with epoch fencing; it dominates lease-based alternatives because it records causality directly rather than inferring it from timeouts, and the exact durable records are the intent entry and the seal entry.\nMECHANISM: Before acquiring the workspace, the process appends an intent record (problem_id, epoch, workspace_path, process_id) to a durable append-only log. Upon completion, it appends a seal record containing the output evidence hash. Reclamation performs a log-scan: any workspace with an intent but no matching seal, whose holding process has been confirmed dead by a liveness oracle, is reclaimed. The causal mechanism is that the log provides a recoverable total order of intent and seal events, so displacement is always preceded by a visible gap (intent without seal), never silent. Adj\n[268bf8aaf335] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: A content-addressed derivation cache with durable provenance anchors achieves equivalent safety without requiring a global log, because evidence identity is decoupled from workspace location; the exact durable record is the (problem_id, input_hash, output_hash) provenance anchor.\nMECHANISM: The deterministic workspace implies reproducibility. The protocol durably records a provenance anchor mapping problem_id to (input_hash, output_hash) before declaring evidence complete. The actual workspace directory is treated as ephemeral scratch. Reclamation is always safe because there is no completed evidence in the workspace—only a cache that can be rebuilt. Displacement of the workspace is therefore never displacement of evidence. The causal mechanism is the cryptographic commitment in the provenance anchor, which binds the evidence to its causal history\n[58b49350d38c] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Skeptical best-effort: absolute 'never' is impossible without synchronous consensus; the safest practical protocol is a durable lease table with explicit revocation tokens, adjudicated against log-based protocols by its superior availability under partition.\nMECHANISM: A lease manager durably records (workspace_id, holder_pid, issued_timestamp, ttl, revocation_flag). The process must heartbeat to renew. Reclamation queries the lease table and a process liveness oracle; if both confirm expiry or death, it issues a revocation token and reclaims. The causal mechanism is timeout-driven state transition. It adjudicates against the intent-seal log: while the log protocol is safer in consistent histories, it fails closed under partition (stalls reclamation), whereas the lease protocol can make progress with bounded uncertainty. Therefore, where evidence \n[07071810050f] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: A unifying generational tri-state manifest (active/staging/sealed) with downstream dependency pinning subsumes both log and lease protocols by making causal dependencies explicit in the workspace metadata itself; the exact durable record is the manifest containing generation, state, and downstream references.\nMECHANISM: The durable record is a manifest per problem containing generation number, state, and a list of downstream evidence refs that consume this workspace. State transitions: active → staging (completion in progress) → sealed (completed). Reclamation first marks staging workspaces as 'zombie', waits for downstream dependency resolution, then reclaims only if no downstream ref exists and the holding process is dead. The causal mechanism is reference counting over the derivation graph. Adjudication: this protocol is safer than pure lease o\n[7d1ee64a4b20] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: Crash-only software with resurrection logs: safety is achieved by never distinguishing between crash and restart; the durable record is a birth certificate that enables idempotent workspace reconstruction rather than hazardous reclamation.\nMECHANISM: On every startup, the process writes a birth certificate (problem_id, deterministic_seed, workspace_path, incarnation_number) to durable storage. All writes to the workspace are idempotent and logged in a redo log. 'Reclamation' is simply a new process incarnation reading the birth certificate and replaying the log to determine if evidence was already completed. If completed, the workspace is re-adopted; if not, it is reconstructed. The causal mechanism is deterministic replay: the workspace is not a resource to reclaim but a function of the durable birth certificate and log. This protocol never displ\n[48afe33acd70] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: An epistemic, data-centric protocol using a distributed Merkle witness tree shifts safety from process liveness to content acknowledgment; the exact durable records are Merkle roots and inclusion proofs.\nMECHANISM: Rather than tracking process liveness, the protocol requires that any completed evidence be hashed and that hash inserted into a Merkle tree witnessed by multiple observers. Reclamation performs a 'corpse examination': it hashes the orphaned workspace and checks inclusion in the Merkle tree. If present, evidence is completed and must not be displaced. If absent, reclamation proceeds. The causal mechanism shifts from process-centric to data-centric: a workspace is safe not because its creator is alive but because its content is globally acknowledged. Adjudication: where evidence shows observer crash rates are lower than process crash rat\n[6a95e6c7a4e9] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: The safest deterministic reclamation protocol is an append-only intent-and-seal log with epoch fencing and monotonic-read witnesses; it dominates lease-based rivals because it records completion before reclamation can observe death, and the exact durable record must contain the ordered tuple (problem-id, content-addressed evidence-hash, seal-epoch, upstream-dependency-hashes, holder-intent-bit).\nMECHANISM: Before releasing the workspace, the holder appends a seal entry to a causally-ordered log. The reclaimer scans backward from the log tail for a seal entry matching the workspace problem-id; if none is visible in the reclaimer's causal cut, reclamation is forbidden. Epoch fencing prevents a zombie holder from sealing after the workspace has been reclaimed and reissued. The required durable record is therefore the seal entry itself plus the log's \n[6f1a7c780e46] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: A content-addressed derivation cache with durable provenance anchors achieves equivalent safety without a global log, provided the anchor record includes the full dependency closure, a tombstone-resistant witness attestation, and a seal-of-derivation signed by the workspace holder.\nMECHANISM: Completed evidence is externalized as a content-addressed object. The holder writes a provenance anchor to a durable, replicated key-value store keyed by problem-id, containing (content-hash, input-hashes, timestamp-witness, holder-epoch). Reclamation only proceeds if a causal cut shows no anchor for the problem-id and the holder's heartbeat lease has expired. Because the evidence is content-addressed, displacement is detectable: recompute yields the same hash, revealing a silent displacement after the fact.\n[d0c1cb17e452] (school: school-1)\nCLAIM: Treating the workspace as a causal broadcast medium where every write emits a content-hash witness into a Merkle DAG shifts reclamation from a liveness test to a data-availability query; safety is preserved if and only if the protocol durably records the root hash of the problem-specific witness subtree and a quorum certificate before any reclamation decision.\nMECHANISM: The holder asynchronously publishes workspace state transitions as Merkle leaves; completion is marked by sealing the subtree root into a globally visible DAG node. The reclaimer queries DAG reachability rather than process status. The required durable record is the set of DAG root hashes witnessed by a quorum of reclaimer-adjacent nodes, ensuring that even if 