ion-209e3154673cd752fd6dc26d2ddbbf90"}],"schema":"bridge.evidence-pack.v1","source_terminal_commitment_ref":"sha256:8885dd5179c8ce127bcb8813dd7f3364cd5b783fbae704c5b092fbb6547125ed","survivors":[{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-209e3154673cd752fd6dc26d2ddbbf90"],"artifact_ref":"673a9872b715a5f0f9080ecde6ef17f13ee6b5fe5e1f04f615e9e14a9a073346","citation_id":"673a9872b715","claim":"The safest protocol is an append-only intent-and-seal log with epoch fencing; it dominates lease-based alternatives because it records causality directly rather than inferring it from timeouts, and the exact durable records are the intent entry and the seal entry.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"Before acquiring the workspace, the process appends an intent record (problem_id, epoch, workspace_path, process_id) to a durable append-only log. Upon completion, it appends a seal record containing the output evidence hash. Reclamation performs a log-scan: any workspace with an intent but no matching seal, whose holding process has been confirmed dead by a liveness oracle, is reclaimed. The causal mechanism is that the log provides a recoverable total order of intent and seal events, so displacement is always preceded by a visible gap (intent without seal), never silent. Adjudication against lease protocols: where the evidence shows lease expiry can race with seal writes, the log protocol wins because seal visibility is immediate to readers of the log, whereas lease expiry is a timeout inference.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[673a9872b715] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: The safest protocol is an append-only intent-and-seal log with epoch fencing; it dominates lease-based alternatives because it records causality directly rather than inferring it from timeouts, and the exact durable records are the intent entry and the seal entry.\nMECHANISM: Before acquiring the workspace, the process appends an intent record (problem_id, epoch, workspace_path, process_id) to a durable append-only log. Upon completion, it appends a seal record containing the output evidence hash. Reclamation performs a log-scan: any workspace with an intent but no matching seal, whose holding process has been confirmed dead by a liveness oracle, is reclaimed. The causal mechanism is that the log provides a recoverable total order of intent and seal events, so displacement is always preceded by a visible gap (intent without seal), never silent. Adj","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-209e3154673cd752fd6dc26d2ddbbf90"],"artifact_ref":"268bf8aaf33529f3939ef2f9b145d03a719924228e01b5939fb0a1cd890ff698","citation_id":"268bf8aaf335","claim":"A content-addressed derivation cache with durable provenance anchors achieves equivalent safety without requiring a global log, because evidence identity is decoupled from workspace location; the exact durable record is the (problem_id, input_hash, output_hash) provenance anchor.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"The deterministic workspace implies reproducibility. The protocol durably records a provenance anchor mapping problem_id to (input_hash, output_hash) before declaring evidence complete. The actual workspace directory is treated as ephemeral scratch. Reclamation is always safe because there is no completed evidence in the workspace—only a cache that can be rebuilt. Displacement of the workspace is therefore never displacement of evidence. The causal mechanism is the cryptographic commitment in the provenance anchor, which binds the evidence to its causal history independent of storage location. Adjudication: this dominates log-based protocols when input data is immutable and recomputation is cheaper than log coordination, but loses when determinism is imperfect.","overturn_conditions":[],"rendered_text":"[268bf8aaf335] (school: school-0)\nCLAIM: A content-addressed derivation cache with durable provenance anchors achieves equivalent safety without requiring a global log, because evidence identity is decoupled from workspace location; the exact durable record is the (problem_id, input_hash, output_hash) provenance anchor.\nMECHANISM: The deterministic workspace implies reproducibility. The protocol durably records a provenance anchor mapping problem_id to (input_hash, output_hash) before declaring evidence complete. The actual workspace directory is treated as ephemeral scratch. Reclamation is always safe because there is no completed evidence in the workspace—only a cache that can be rebuilt. Displacement of the workspace is therefore never displacement of evidence. The causal mechanism is the cryptographic commitment in the provenance anchor, which binds the evidence to its causal history","school":"school-0"},{"addressed_problem_refs":["question-209e3154673cd752fd6dc26d2ddbbf90"],"artifact_ref":"58b49350d38c98fbc34417a51cc47de21d6465cbbbc90bc9fc0c5c3e7a3ad2dc","citation_id":"58b49350d38c","claim":"Skeptical best-effort: absolute 'never' is impossible without synchronous consensus; the safest practical protocol is a durable lease table with explicit revocation tokens, adjudicated against log-based protocols by its superior availability under partition.","lineage":{"dependence_refs":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mention_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"source_refs":[],"trace_refs":[],"warrant_refs":[]},"mechanism":"A lease manager durably records (workspace_id, holder_pid, issued_timestamp, ttl, revocation_flag). The process must heartbeat to renew. Reclamation queries the lease table and a process liveness oracle; if both confirm expiry or death, it issues a revocation token and reclaims. The causal mechanism is